<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Hungarian translation.

    The source is English, in `values/`, and this file is a translation of it —
    never the place a string is invented. The `name` attributes, the
    `tools:ignore` attributes and the plural categories are left exactly as
    they stand. Hungarian has two categories, `one` and `other`, and only a
    bare 1 falls into `one`.

    **A numeral leaves the noun behind it in the singular** — „3 kerékpár”,
    never „3 kerékpárok” — so both categories of a plural read the same way
    here. Both are still written out: `one` is what a count of 1 resolves to,
    and a wrong `one` would be read every time a station holds a single bike.

    **The definite article changes with the sound that follows** — *a* before a
    consonant, *az* before a vowel — and a placeholder makes that
    unpredictable: a station may be „a Batthyány tér”, a network „az MOL Bubi”.
    Not one line in this file puts an article in front of a `%…$s`.

    **Hungarian is agglutinative**, and a case ending glued onto a placeholder
    would be wrong half the time. Not one line does that either: the ending
    falls on a noun this file owns — *állomásig*, *hálózatnak*, *területen* —
    and the name follows it in apposition, usually behind a colon. Read
    docs/hu-glossary.md before rewording any of them; the table there lists
    every line that carries the weight.

    Typography: quotation marks are „ … ”; no space stands before `:` or `;`;
    the dash that breaks a sentence is – with a space on either side, not the
    em dash the English file uses. The apostrophe, where a network's name
    carries one (Vélib’, Vélo’v, V’lille), is ’ (U+2019) and never the straight
    quote, which is why nothing here is escaped.

    Register: controls are nominal — *Letöltés*, *Törlés*, *Megjelenítés* —
    like Android's own Hungarian; running prose addresses the reader informally
    (*tegezés*). The glossary says why that departs from Android's *magázás*.
    See CONTRIBUTING.md.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <string name="app_name">Roue Libre</string>

    <!-- Main screen: the station list. -->
    <string name="stations_title">Állomások</string>

    <!-- The number of bikes available to borrow at a station. A Hungarian
         numeral leaves its noun in the singular, so both categories read
         „%1$d kerékpár”. -->
    <plurals name="bikes_available">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d kerékpár</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d kerékpár</item>
    </plurals>

    <!-- The number of free docks to return a bike into. -->
    <plurals name="docks_available">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d szabad hely</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d szabad hely</item>
    </plurals>

    <!-- Spoken by screen readers on a station's indicator.
         Example: „Batthyány tér, 6 kerékpár, 26 szabad hely”. -->
    <string name="station_content_description">%1$s, %2$s, %3$s</string>
    <string name="station_out_of_service">Nem működik</string>
    <string name="station_out_of_service_since">%1$s · utolsó jelzés %2$s</string>
    <string name="station_availability_unknown">Ismeretlen elérhetőség</string>

    <!-- What a station is called when its feed publishes no name for it and no
         street to stand in for one. -->
